    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d585">

                <pb facs="0765" n="753" xml:id="pg753"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/12–1845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-01-05T18:00:00-05:00">January 5, 1946—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>60. If favorable opportunity presents itself you may wish to inform M.
                        Bidault<anchor n="4" xml:id="d585-f1"/> or some other appropriate Foreign
                    Ministry official that Dept’s views re Anglo-French Levant agreement are those
                    contained in first three paragraphs of Dept’s 5975, Dec. 20, 9 p.m.<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d585-f2"/> As stated in Dept’s 6069, Dec. 28, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d585-f3"/> these views were made clear, at Mr. Bevin’s<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d585-f4"/> request, to Brit Govt which has indicated that substance
                    thereof was communicated to French.</p>
                <p>Oral communication received from Brit Emb here quotes Mr. Bevin as stating in
                    fact that our views enabled Brit “to represent very forcibly to French Govt
                    objections which would be felt in America to the phrase ‘the leading role’
                    (contained in first draft agreement shown Dept) and as a result we were able
                    eventually to induce French Govt to agree to revised wording which is not open
                    to similar objection”.</p>
                <p>Sent Paris. Repeated London and Beirut for Damascus.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d585-f1" n="4" xml:id="d585-f1-content">
                <p>Georges Bidault, French Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d585-f2" n="5" xml:id="d585-f2-content">
                <p>This was a repeat of telegram 413 to Beirut, <ref target="frus1945v08#pg_1184">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>, p. 1184</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d585-f3" n="6" xml:id="d585-f3-content">
                <p>This was a repeat of telegram 420 to Beirut, <ref target="frus1945v08#pg_1187">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, p. 1187</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d585-f4" n="7" xml:id="d585-f4-content">
                <p>Ernest Bevin, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
